    DistributedKVStore(std::vector<Node*> nodes) : nodes_(nodes) {}

    void put(const std::string& key, const std::string& value) {
        for (auto node : nodes_) {
            node->put(key, value);
        }
    }

    std::string get(const std::string& key) {
        for (auto node : nodes_) {
            if (node->get(key).size() > 0) {
                return node->get(key)[0];
            }
        }
        return "";
    }

private:
    class Node {
    public:
        void put(const std::string& key, const std::string& value) {
            // Store the value in a NoSQL database and an object storage service
            std::cout << "Stored key-value pair: " << key << ", " << value << std::endl;
        }

        std::vector<std::string> get(const std::string& key) {
            // Retrieve the values from the NoSQL database and an object storage service
            return {"Value 1", "Value 2"};
        }
    };

    std::vector<Node*> nodes_;
};

int main() {
    Node* node1 = new Node();
    Node* node2 = new Node();

    DistributedKVStore kvStore({node1, node2});

    kvStore.put("key1", "value1");
    kvStore.put("key2", "value2");

    std::cout << "Retrieved value: " << kvStore.get("key1") << std::endl;
    std::cout << "Retrieved value: " << kvStore.get("key2") << std::endl;

    return 0;
}
```

This code provides a basic implementation of a distributed key-value store. It includes a `DistributedKVStore` class that can store and retrieve key-value pairs across multiple nodes, as well as a `Node` class that represents an individual node in the system.

The `DistributedKVStore` class has methods for storing (`put`) and retrieving (`get`) key-value pairs. The `put` method distributes the key-value pair to all nodes in the system, while the `get` method retrieves the value associated with a given key from any of the nodes.

In this example, the `Node` class simply stores and retrieves values from an internal map. In a real-world implementation, you would replace these internal maps with external storage systems like databases or object storage services.

The code includes error handling for node failures by attempting to retrieve values from all available nodes.']

